#ifndef _VXCXX_MEMORY
#define _VXCXX_MEMORY

/*
 * <memory> — who owns what.
 *
 * ---- unique_ptr costs nothing ----
 *
 * It holds one pointer and has no virtual functions, so it is the same
 * size as the pointer it wraps and every one of its operations compiles
 * to what the raw pointer would have. What it adds is a destructor,
 * which is the whole point: a raw pointer that goes out of scope on an
 * early return leaks, and this one does not, and there is no way to
 * write the leak.
 *
 * ---- shared_ptr costs a word and an atomic ----
 *
 * Two pointers — the object and a control block — and every copy is an
 * atomic increment. The count is atomic and not a plain integer because
 * two threads copying the same shared_ptr are not a rare case; they are
 * what a shared_ptr is *for*, and a non-atomic count under that is a
 * double free that appears once every few hours.
 *
 * The weak count is kept beside it so that a weak_ptr can outlive the
 * object without keeping it alive, which is how a cycle is broken.
 *
 * ---- make_shared makes one allocation ----
 *
 * `shared_ptr<T>(new T(...))` is two: the object and the control block.
 * make_shared puts them in one, which halves the allocations and puts
 * the count on the same cache line as the object. The cost is that the
 * storage is only released when the last *weak* reference goes, since
 * the block and the object are one block — which is the trade every
 * implementation makes and is worth knowing if a weak_ptr is being held
 * to something large.
 */

#include <cstddef>
#include <type_traits>
#include <utility>
#include <new>
